>>> Explain step by step, in about 150 words, why the sky is blue during the day and red at sunset.

[answer]
During the day, sunlight travels through the atmosphere in all directions. As it hits the air molecules, it scatters shorter wavelengths (blue and violet) more effectively than longer ones (red). Since our eyes are more sensitive to blue, the sky appears blue.

At sunset, the sun is lower on the horizon, causing sunlight to travel through a much thicker layer of atmosphere. This increased path length scatters almost all blue light away from the viewer. The remaining red and orange wavelengths pass through to reach our eyes, creating the vibrant sunset colors.
